\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Decision 1 \'97 The HAL Core boundary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We need to decide what it actually means for something to be part of 
\f0\b \strokec2 HAL Core
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
Without a strict boundary, Core will gradually absorb every useful feature: speech, embeddings, inference, file processing, browser automation, home control, and eventually half the system. That creates a centralized monolith that is difficult to scale, recover, secure, or evolve.\
But pushing too much onto nodes creates the opposite problem: HAL\'92s judgment and identity become fragmented across machines.\
The boundary must preserve our central principle:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core is the brain. Nodes are appendages.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
The key distinction is not lightweight work versus heavyweight work. It is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Authority versus execution.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The main options\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 A. Thin coordinator
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Core only routes requests and monitors nodes.\
This is simple, but too weak. Strategic behavior would leak into individual services and nodes. HAL would become a model router rather than an executive system.\

\f0\b \strokec2 B. Centralized application
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Core owns orchestration and performs many capabilities locally whenever possible.\
This is convenient early on, but it will become a bottleneck and a single failure domain. Capability implementation would become entangled with governance.\

\f0\b \strokec2 C. Executive control plane
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Core owns decisions, state, policy, and evaluation. Nodes and external services perform capability execution.\
This cleanly separates HAL\'92s identity from the machinery it uses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Choose 
\f0\b \strokec2 C: HAL Core as an executive control plane
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
HAL Core should own every authoritative decision:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What the user intended\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What objective is active\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What workflow should run\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Which capabilities are required\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Which execution target receives work\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Whether the result is acceptable\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What HAL believes and remembers\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What actions are permitted\
\ls17\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Whether an objective is complete\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Core may run small internal models and ordinary CPU work when they support executive functions such as intent classification or memory extraction. But those models remain replaceable internal implementations\'97not user-facing capabilities.\
HAL Core should 
\f0\b \strokec2 not
\f1\b0 \strokec2  perform substantial capability execution merely because the local machine can handle it.\
For example, Core may parse a request, but it should not become the video-processing server. It may select an embedding capability, but it should not permanently own the embedding runtime. It may coordinate a backup, but the storage service performs the data movement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The boundary test\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 For every proposed Core component, ask:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Does this component decide what HAL should do, preserve authoritative state, enforce governance, or judge the outcome?
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
If yes, it probably belongs in Core.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Does it primarily perform the requested work?
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
If yes, it belongs on a node or behind a capability adapter.\
There is one important nuance: 
\f0\b \strokec2 deployment location does not determine architectural ownership
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
A capability worker could initially run on the same physical machine as HAL Core for convenience. Architecturally, it is still a worker and must communicate through the same contracts as a remote node. That prevents early deployment shortcuts from becoming permanent coupling.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Proposed locked decision\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core is the authoritative executive control plane of HAL. It owns intent, objectives, workflows, policy, trust, scheduling, memory coordination, evaluation, and desired state. It may use lightweight replaceable models for executive functions, but it does not own substantive capability execution. All workers\'97including those colocated on the Core machine\'97operate through explicit capability contracts and possess no independent strategic authority.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
My recommendation is to lock that as the first rule of the HAL Design Specification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 This makes sense and i want to take your reco, but i have a question.\
Hypothetically, if in the future i have 10 gx10 units. Could HAL core end up on one of those nodes, but not actually hold any inference, speech recognition, or processing power if there are better models and one is warmed up already?\
What im asking really is\'97will HAL core eventually become too powerful and i need to upgrade the hardware, or can some of it's intended functionality be passed to nodes to for processing while it retains it's judgement and authority over the workflow?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Yes \'97 and that distinction is exactly the right one.\
HAL Core can remain the 
\f0\b \strokec2 authority
\f1\b0 \strokec2  without being the place where all computation happens.\
The clean way to think about it is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core owns decisions.\strokec2 \uc0\u8232 \strokec2 Nodes provide computation.\strokec2 \
So even if HAL Core physically runs on one GX10, it does not need to use that machine\'92s GPU for inference, speech recognition, embeddings, vision, or other heavy work. It can schedule those jobs onto whichever node has the best available model, the right hardware, the warmest cache, or the lowest latency.\
That means a future ten-node system could look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 GX10-1\
HAL Core control plane\
Lightweight executive models\
No large inference workload\
\
GX10-2 to GX10-10\
Reasoning\
Speech\
Vision\
Embeddings\
Coding\
Background jobs\
Model serving\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Or HAL Core could run on a smaller, more reliable machine entirely, while all ten GX10s remain pure workers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The problem we are solving\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We need to prevent two different things from becoming accidentally coupled:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Strategic authority
\f1\b0 \
\ls18\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Compute capacity
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 If those are coupled, then every time HAL needs a better model, more VRAM, or more throughput, you would have to scale or replace the Core itself.\
That would be a bad design.\
Instead, the Core should issue a request like:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 Capability: speech.transcribe\
Requirements:\
- language: English\
- max latency: 400 ms\
- quality tier: high\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 The scheduler then chooses the best execution target.\
The Core does not care whether that capability runs on the local GPU, another GX10, a CPU service, or some future accelerator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What may be delegated\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A lot of Core-related computation can be delegated.\
For example:\
