\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Larger event volumes\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More users\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More memory queries\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More node telemetry\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More scheduling decisions\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More audit and provenance data\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Those workloads usually scale with CPU, RAM, storage performance, and network reliability\'97not massive GPU capacity.\
And even those can be scaled horizontally later.\
For example, HAL Core could eventually become a small cluster:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core Cluster\

\f4 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  API and identity replicas\

\f4 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  Workflow engine replicas\

\f4 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  Scheduler replicas\

\f4 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  Memory services\

\f4 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  Policy and trust services\

\f4 \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472 
\f3  Consensus-backed authoritative state\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Logically, it is still one Core.\
Physically, it may be several machines.\
That gives us an important architectural principle:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core is a logical authority, not necessarily a single physical computer.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We should slightly refine the boundary we were about to lock.\
I recommend this wording:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core is the authoritative executive control plane of HAL. It owns judgment, policy, workflow state, memory authority, scheduling, and completion decisions. Computational work supporting those responsibilities may be delegated through capability contracts, but delegated workers may only return observations, proposals, or results. They may not independently mutate authoritative state or exercise strategic authority.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
And I would add a second rule:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 The logical HAL Core must remain independent of the hardware on which it runs. It may be deployed on one machine, colocated with workers, or distributed across multiple machines without changing its authority model.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
That gives you exactly the future you described: ten GX10s, warm models everywhere, and HAL Core choosing the best available computation without becoming the GPU furnace in the corner.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
