\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decision 2 \'97 HAL Core Internal Structure\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 HAL Core will begin as an extractable modular monolith. Every logical service must own its state, expose versioned contracts, support location-transparent communication, provide independent packaging, and include a standalone deployment manifest. A service must be capable of running in-process, out-of-process, or remotely without changing its domain logic or authority.
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b HAL may monitor service-level performance and recommend extraction when evidence shows that isolation, scaling, reliability, or security would improve. With the Founder\'92s explicit approval, HAL may automatically provision, validate, shadow, migrate, cut over, and roll back the service. Extraction must remain an operational topology change rather than an architectural rewrite.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decision 3 \'97 How should HAL Core services communicate?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We need a communication model that supports two very different kinds of interaction:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Immediate requests
\f1\b0 , where one service needs an answer now\
\ls22\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 State changes
\f1\b0 , where other services may need to react independently\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 If everything is synchronous, HAL becomes tightly coupled and brittle.\
If everything is event-driven, simple operations become awkward, hard to trace, and eventually consistent when they should not be.\
So the real question is not \'93RPC or events?\'94\
It is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Which interactions require a direct answer, and which should become durable facts about what happened?
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The main options\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 A. Synchronous calls everywhere
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Simple at first, but creates deep dependency chains. One slow service can stall the entire workflow.\

\f0\b \strokec2 B. Events everywhere
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Excellent decoupling, but poor for operations that require a definitive response, such as permission checks or retrieving authoritative workflow state.\

\f0\b \strokec2 C. Hybrid commands, queries, and events
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Use each interaction type for a specific purpose.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Choose 
\f0\b \strokec2 C: a hybrid CQE model
\f1\b0 \strokec2 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Commands
\f1\b0  request a state change.\
\ls23\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Queries
\f1\b0  retrieve authoritative state without changing it.\
\ls23\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Events
\f1\b0  record that something has happened.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 Command:\
CreateObjective\
\
Query:\
GetObjectiveStatus\
\
Event:\
ObjectiveCreated\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 This produces a very clean rule:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Commands express intent.\strokec2 \uc0\u8232 \strokec2 Queries request facts.\strokec2 \uc0\u8232 \strokec2 Events describe history.\strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Communication behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Inside the initial modular monolith, commands and queries may execute in-process through location-transparent service interfaces.\
Events should still be written to a durable event journal before being distributed.\
When a module is extracted:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Commands and queries transparently become remote calls.\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Events continue through the same durable event system.\
\ls24\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Domain logic remains unchanged.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Why events must be durable\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Events are more than notifications in HAL.\
They provide:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls25\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Auditability\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Replay\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Recovery\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Provenance\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observability\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workflow reconstruction\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Debugging\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Future subscribers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 IntentRecognized\
ObjectiveCreated\
WorkflowPlanned\
CapabilityScheduled\
ExecutionCompleted\
EvaluationFailed\
RevisionRequested\
ObjectiveCompleted\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 That sequence becomes a reliable history of what HAL did and why.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 One important guardrail\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The event stream must not become HAL\'92s hidden source of truth for everything.\
Each service owns its current authoritative state.\
The event journal records how that state changed.\
That avoids the complexity of full event sourcing while preserving the benefits of a durable history.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Proposed decision to lock\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core will use a hybrid command, query, and event communication model. Commands request authoritative state changes, queries retrieve authoritative state, and events record completed facts. Commands and queries must use location-transparent, versioned service contracts. Events must be durable, ordered within their relevant scope, replayable, and stored in the HAL Event Journal.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Each service remains the authoritative owner of its current state. The Event Journal records state transitions and system history but will not, by default, be the sole source of truth for reconstructing all service state.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
My recommendation is to lock this hybrid model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
