\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decision 3 \'97 HAL Core Communication Model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL Core will use a hybrid command, query, and event communication model. Commands request authoritative state changes, queries retrieve authoritative state, and events record completed facts. Commands and queries must use location-transparent, versioned service contracts. Events must be durable, ordered within their relevant scope, replayable, and stored in the HAL Event Journal.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Each service remains the authoritative owner of its current state. The Event Journal records state transitions and system history but will not, by default, be the sole source of truth for reconstructing all service state.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decision 4 \'97 What should the HAL Event Journal guarantee?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We have decided that events are durable and replayable. Now we need to define what that actually means.\
This is where many distributed systems become unreliable. Terms like \'93exactly once,\'94 \'93ordered,\'94 and \'93replayable\'94 sound precise, but they hide difficult tradeoffs.\
We need clear guarantees for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Duplicate events\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Events arriving out of order\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Consumers crashing halfway through work\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Replaying old history\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Schema changes\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Network partitions\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One event triggering another command\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The goal is not to pretend failures never happen. It is to make failures safe and explainable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The main options\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 A. Exactly-once delivery
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Attractive in theory, but usually expensive, fragile, and misleading across service boundaries.\

\f0\b \strokec2 B. At-most-once delivery
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Simple, but events may be lost. That is unacceptable for HAL\'92s audit and workflow history.\

\f0\b \strokec2 C. At-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Events may occasionally be delivered more than once, but none are silently lost. Consumers are designed so reprocessing the same event does not corrupt state.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Choose 
\f0\b \strokec2 C: at-least-once delivery with strong idempotency
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
Each event should contain:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls27\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A globally unique event ID\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Event type and schema version\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Producer service\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Timestamp\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Correlation ID for the overall request or objective\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Causation ID for the command or event that produced it\
