\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Ordering\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 We should not promise one total order across all of HAL. That would limit scalability and create unnecessary coordination.\
Instead:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Events must be ordered within the domain object or workflow where order matters.\strokec2 \
For example, the events for one objective must preserve order:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 ObjectiveCreated\
WorkflowPlanned\
ExecutionStarted\
EvaluationCompleted\
ObjectiveCompleted\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Events from unrelated objectives do not need a universal sequence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Replay\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Replay should support two modes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Operational replay
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Redeliver missed events to recover a consumer.\

\f0\b \strokec2 Analytical replay
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Reprocess historical events into a new projection, benchmark, or observability tool.\
Replay must not accidentally repeat external side effects. Sending an email twice because an event was replayed would be a fairly memorable bug.\
Any side-effecting action must therefore use an idempotency key and record its completion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Schema evolution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Events are permanent history, so their schemas cannot be treated casually.\
My recommendation:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Events are immutable once published.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Existing event meanings never change.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Breaking changes create a new event version.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Consumers must support a defined compatibility window.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Upcasters may translate older events into current internal representations.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The original event remains preserved unchanged.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Proposed decision to lock\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 The HAL Event Journal will provide durable at-least-once event delivery. All event consumers must be idempotent, and all producers must use a transactional outbox or equivalent atomic publication mechanism. HAL will not claim end-to-end exactly-once execution.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Events will be ordered within the smallest relevant authority scope, such as an objective, workflow, node, or managed resource, rather than through one global system order. Every event will include identity, schema version, correlation, causation, sequence, actor, authorization, provenance, and integrity metadata.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Published events are immutable. Replay must be safe, observable, and incapable of unintentionally repeating external side effects. Event evolution will use versioning and compatibility mechanisms while preserving the original historical record.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
My recommendation is to lock this. It gives HAL honest guarantees rather than distributed-systems fairy dust.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
