The CFO owns finance.\
The COO owns operations.\
Everyone collaborates.\
Everyone advises.\
Everyone has visibility.\
But when a financial decision needs to be made, there is one authoritative owner.\
HAL should work exactly the same way.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 In fact, I think we can now define another guiding principle for the Design Specification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 The Principle of Distributed Responsibility\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Responsibility is distributed.\strokec2 \
\strokec2 Authority is singular.\strokec2 \
Those are not the same thing.\
For example:\
The Scheduler might 
\f2\i \strokec2 recommend
\f1\i0 \strokec2  moving a workflow.\
The Quality Authority might 
\f2\i \strokec2 recommend
\f1\i0 \strokec2  retrying it.\
The Memory Authority might 
\f2\i \strokec2 recommend
\f1\i0 \strokec2  promoting a result.\
But only the 
\f0\b \strokec2 Objective Engine
\f1\b0 \strokec2  decides whether the objective changes state.\
Likewise:\
Node Control measures node health.\
The Scheduler uses that information.\
But Node Control alone determines the canonical lifecycle of a node.\
This keeps every subsystem focused on one job, while still enabling rich collaboration.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 I also think we've stumbled onto a pattern that's going to repeat throughout HAL:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Identity
\f1\b0  is singular.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Execution
\f1\b0  is distributed.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Authority
\f1\b0  is singular.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Computation
\f1\b0  is distributed.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Memory ownership
\f1\b0  is singular.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Memory access
\f1\b0  is distributed.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Policy
\f1\b0  is singular.\
\ls32\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Recommendations
\f1\b0  are distributed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 There's a beautiful consistency emerging.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Decision 5 is officially locked\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL follows the Single Authority Principle. Every durable piece of system state has exactly one authoritative owner. Only the owning service may mutate that state. All other services interact with it through commands and queries, and may maintain read-only projections or caches that are explicitly treated as non-authoritative.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decision 6 \'97 How should HAL identify everything?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This may sound mundane, but I think it's one of the highest-leverage decisions we'll make.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL is going to manage a lot of things:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls33\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Users\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Objectives\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Conversations\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Memories\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Nodes\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Capabilities\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Models\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workflows\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Events\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tools\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Devices\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Automations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Every one of those needs an identity.\
The easy answer is "just use UUIDs."\
I don't think that's the right answer.\
I have a recommendation that I think will make HAL dramatically easier to reason about, debug, and even converse with. And I think it will become one of those invisible design decisions that pays dividends for years.\
I think you'll like this one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 what's the reco?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Decision 6 \'97 Identity Model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL needs identifiers that work equally well for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls34\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Machines\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 APIs\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Logs\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Human conversation\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Audits\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Migrations\
\ls34\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Distributed services\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Pure UUIDs are excellent for uniqueness and terrible for humans. Names are excellent for humans and terrible as permanent identity because names change.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every durable entity gets 
\f0\b \strokec2 two identities
\f1\b0 \strokec2 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls35\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Immutable system ID
\f1\b0 \
\ls35\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Human-readable stable handle
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 System ID:\
01JZ8Q7V3M6C9A2F4K1X8R0N5P\
\
Handle:\
objective:hal-core-design\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 The immutable ID is the true identity.\
The handle is how humans and most logs refer to it.\
A display name can change freely:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 Display name:\
HAL Core Architecture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 So each object effectively has:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 id            immutable\
handle        stable and unique within a namespace\
display_name  editable\
aliases       historical or conversational names\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Why this is better than UUID-only\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL can say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93The bottleneck is 
\f3\fs26 \strokec2 service:scheduler
\f1\fs24 \strokec2 .\'94\strokec2 \
Instead of:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93The bottleneck is 
\f3\fs26 \strokec2 8dd47f42-6a93-4af7...
\f1\fs24 \strokec2 .\'94\strokec2 \
But renaming \'93Scheduler\'94 to \'93Execution Scheduler\'94 does not break references, because neither the immutable ID nor its stable handle needs to change.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 ID format\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 I recommend 
\f0\b \strokec2 UUIDv7 or ULID-style sortable identifiers
\f1\b0 \strokec2 , with a preference for 
\f0\b \strokec2 UUIDv7
\f1\b0 \strokec2  if the implementation ecosystem supports it well.\
They are:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls36\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Globally unique\
\ls36\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Time-sortable\
\ls36\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Suitable for distributed generation\
\ls36\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Better for database indexes than random UUIDv4 values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Humans should rarely see the raw ID.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Namespaced handles\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Handles should be explicit:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 user:founder\
node:gx10-01\
service:objective-engine\
capability:speech.transcribe\
model:whisper-large-v3\
objective:hal-design-spec\
policy:constitutional-amendment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 This avoids ambiguity and makes logs almost self-explanatory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Founder identity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Your constitutional authority must attach to an immutable identity\'97not merely a username, face, voiceprint, or email.\
For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 user:founder\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 That handle maps to your immutable principal ID.\
Authentication methods may change over time, but the principal remains the same. Your wife or child may have administrator privileges elsewhere without inheriting constitutional authority.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Proposed decision to lock\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Every durable HAL entity will have an immutable globally unique system identifier, a namespaced human-readable stable handle, and an editable display name. System identifiers will use a time-sortable distributed format such as UUIDv7. Handles may be renamed only through an audited migration, while aliases preserve historical references. Display names may change without affecting identity.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Authorization and constitutional authority attach exclusively to immutable principal identities, never to mutable usernames, devices, biometric methods, household roles, or display names.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
That is my recommendation. It gives HAL machine-grade identity without making the system miserable for humans.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
