\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Decision 10 \'97 What Is a Capability?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1. The problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL needs a stable way to describe 
\f0\b \strokec2 what it can do
\f1\b0 \strokec2  without binding that ability to any specific model, tool, service, node, vendor, or implementation.\
For example, \'93summarize a document\'94 might be fulfilled by:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls57\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 a local language model\
\ls57\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 a cloud model\
\ls57\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 a specialized summarization service\
\ls57\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 a workflow combining retrieval, synthesis, and verification\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The user should request the outcome.\
HAL should decide how to produce it.\
So a capability cannot simply mean \'93an API endpoint\'94 or \'93a model function.\'94 Those are implementations. They will change constantly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Architectural options\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Option A \'97 Tools are capabilities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 A capability is directly equivalent to a tool or API.\
Example:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 gmail.send_email
\f1\fs24 \
\ls58\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ollama.generate
\f1\fs24 \
\ls58\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 docker.deploy
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 This is simple, but it exposes implementation details throughout the system. Replacing a tool changes everything that depends on it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Option B \'97 Services are capabilities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Each internal service advertises what it provides.\
This is cleaner, but still couples HAL\'92s understanding of ability to its current service topology. Extracting or replacing services changes the capability model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Option C \'97 Models are capabilities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Capabilities correspond to what individual models can perform.\
This is the pattern many AI systems use.\
It is also exactly what HAL should avoid. Models are interchangeable resources, not the vocabulary of the operating system.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Option D \'97 Capabilities are implementation-independent contracts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 A capability describes a meaningful outcome HAL can produce, along with its requirements, risks, inputs, outputs, quality expectations, and available implementations.\
