\f6\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u9989 
\f0\b  Decision 10 \'97 Capability Model (LOCKED)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 A capability is an implementation-independent contract representing a meaningful outcome within HAL's operational vocabulary. The existence of a capability does not imply current availability. Availability is determined by the presence of one or more qualified providers capable of realizing that capability under current conditions.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Models, tools, services, nodes, humans, and workflows may act as Capability Providers. Providers declare the conditions under which they can satisfy a capability and are evaluated using observed evidence, including quality, reliability, latency, cost, privacy, resource requirements, and known limitations.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 HAL resolves capability requests into execution plans through the Decision Engine. Users and higher-level workflows depend on capability contracts rather than specific providers or implementations. Capabilities may be composed into higher-order capabilities, enabling HAL to generate, compare, execute, and evaluate alternative execution plans.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Capability contracts classify external effects and risk so that authorization, autonomy, auditing, retry behavior, rollback, and compensation are governed consistently.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

