\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Architectural Options\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Option A \'97 Separate data models for everything\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every subsystem invents its own schema.\
Memory has one format.\
Objectives another.\
Capabilities another.\
Nodes another.\
This is common.\
It also leads to translation layers everywhere and inconsistent reasoning.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Option B \'97 One giant universal schema\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Everything is forced into a single rigid structure.\
Very elegant on paper.\
Usually becomes painful as the system evolves because every new concept stretches the schema.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Option C \'97 Unified Knowledge Graph with typed entities and relationships\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

