\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 5. Proposed Locked Decision\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL shall maintain a Capability Graph representing the compositional relationships among capability contracts. The Capability Graph describes what combinations of capabilities are possible, including semantic input and output compatibility, prerequisites, decomposition relationships, substitutions, constraints, and composition rules. It does not represent a specific unit of planned or executing work.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 For a particular task or objective, HAL shall construct a Plan Graph representing its chosen, context-specific approach. A Plan Graph may contain capability requirements, dependencies, sequencing, parallel branches, decision points, approval gates, evaluation stages, fallback paths, and completion conditions.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 When an approved plan is executed, HAL shall materialize an Execution Graph that binds capability requirements to concrete tasks, providers, nodes, attempts, artifacts, and observed results. Execution Graphs record operational reality and remain distinct from planned intent.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 HAL may maintain reusable Recipes as advisory patterns for generating Plan Graphs. Recipes are learned composition knowledge, not active plans or executable procedures.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \

\f0\b \strokec2 Objective relationships shall remain within the Objective Graph, preserving the distinction between why work exists, how HAL plans to perform it, what HAL is capable of doing, and what actually executes.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

