\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1. Fail open vs. fail closed\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Fail closed\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When a subsystem cannot verify that an action is safe, it blocks the action.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Concrete example: unlocking a door
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
You say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93Hey Otacon, unlock the front door.\'94\strokec2 \
HAL cannot verify your identity because the authentication service is unavailable.\
A fail-closed system responds:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93I can\'92t verify your identity, so I will not unlock the door.\'94\strokec2 \
The safe default is 
\f0\b \strokec2 deny
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
Other fail-closed examples:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls400\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 HAL cannot confirm a user has permission to delete a file, so deletion is blocked.\
\ls400\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Policy Engine is unavailable, so a financial transaction is not executed.\
\ls400\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A node cannot verify the latest governance policy, so it cannot make permanent state changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Fail closed is appropriate when an incorrect action could create a security, safety, financial, privacy, or governance risk.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Fail open\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When a subsystem fails, the surrounding system continues operating in a limited or fallback mode.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Concrete example: voice output
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
You ask HAL a question, but the voice synthesis service fails.\
A fail-open system still gives you the answer in text:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93Voice output is unavailable. Displaying the response instead.\'94\strokec2 \
The failure does not prevent the underlying task from completing.\
Other fail-open examples:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls401\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A recommendation engine is unavailable, so HAL shows unranked results.\
\ls401\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A secondary analytics service fails, so HAL completes the task without the optional analytics.\
\ls401\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A nonessential logging destination is offline, so logs are buffered locally while normal operation continues.\
\ls401\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The preferred large model is unavailable, so HAL uses a smaller local model and clearly reports reduced reasoning quality.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Fail open is appropriate when continuing in a reduced state is safer and more useful than stopping entirely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Important distinction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \'93Fail open\'94 does 
\f0\b \strokec2 not
