\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 End-to-end versus transport encryption\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The architecture should support two layers:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Transport encryption
\f1\b0 \strokec2  protects a connection between adjacent systems, such as a GX10 and the HAL event bus.\

\f0\b \strokec2 End-to-end encryption
\f1\b0 \strokec2  protects the communication object itself so only its intended participants can decrypt the payload\'97even if an intermediary transports or stores it.\
For sensitive communications, governance commands, credentials, private human messages, and restricted evidence, HAL should require end-to-end protection.\
The event bus may need routing metadata, but it should not automatically receive access to the message contents.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 External services\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When HAL communicates with an outside provider, it must use the strongest supported authenticated encryption. If that provider requires access to the plaintext to perform the service, HAL should treat that as an explicit disclosure boundary and apply the relevant data-handling policy before sending anything.\
HAL must never assume that \'93encrypted to the provider\'94 means \'93private from the provider.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Local communication\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Even traffic on your home network should not be trusted merely because it is local.\
A compromised device, misconfigured network, or quarantined node could otherwise inspect or impersonate HAL traffic. Nodes should authenticate one another cryptographically rather than relying on IP addresses or physical network location.\
For communication inside a single trusted process, separate wire encryption may be unnecessary. But as soon as data crosses a process boundary, encryption and authentication should be the default.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Logging and observability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL should preserve communication metadata needed for auditing\'97sender, recipient, time, intent, authorization result, correlation ID\'97without routinely recording decrypted sensitive payloads.\
Where message contents must be retained in the Experience Ledger, they should be encrypted at rest and governed by access classification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Proposed rule for Decision 32\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 All communication between HAL identities must be authenticated, integrity-protected, and encrypted whenever it crosses a process, device, or trust boundary. Sensitive communications must remain encrypted end-to-end to their authorized participants.
