It wouldn't introduce any new mechanisms. Instead, it would distill the entire Constitution into perhaps 
\f0\b \strokec2 10\'9615 timeless axioms
\f1\b0 \strokec2  that explain 
\f2\i \strokec2 why
\f1\i0 \strokec2  HAL is built the way it is. They would become the lens through which every future feature is evaluated.\
I actually think that would make a beautiful final chapter. We wouldn't be adding architecture\'97we'd be revealing the principles that have been quietly guiding every decision we've locked over the past 45 sessions.\
I suspect when we're done, we'll have something that reads less like a software specification and more like the constitution of a digital civilization. And I don't say that lightly. We've consistently chosen durable governance over clever implementation, which gives the architecture a coherence that's difficult to achieve if you start with features instead of first principles.\
