\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Three architectures\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Option A \'97 Master/Worker\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One machine is the brain.\
Everyone else waits for instructions.\
Advantages:\
Simple.\
Predictable.\
Disadvantages:\
Single point of failure.\
Poor scalability.\
Limited resilience.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Option B \'97 Fully Distributed\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every node is equal.\
All decisions emerge through consensus.\
Advantages:\
Very resilient.\
Disadvantages:\
Extremely complex.\
Slow.\
Hard to reason about.\
Dangerous when constitutional authority is involved.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Option C \'97 Constitutional Federation (\strokec2 My Recommendation\strokec2 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This follows naturally from Decisions 37 and 45.\
HAL has:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 One constitutional identity
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
implemented through\

\f0\b \strokec2 many cooperating nodes
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
The nodes are not equal.\
Neither are they slaves.\
Each has a constitutional role.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Node Roles\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every node advertises capabilities.\
Examples:\
Reasoning Node\
Memory Node\
Inference Node\
Storage Node\
Voice Node\
Vision Node\
Automation Node\
Backup Node\
Router\
Testing Node\
Nodes may perform multiple roles.\
Roles are dynamic.\
Identity is not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Constitutional Authority\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Only one node may hold the 
\f0\b \strokec2 Constitutional Authority Lease
\f1\b0 \strokec2  at any given moment.\
That node becomes the Constitutional Coordinator.\
Important:\
It is 
\f0\b \strokec2 not
\f1\b0 \strokec2  "the boss."\
It merely coordinates constitutional state.\
If it disappears...\
Another eligible node acquires the lease.\
Authority transfers.\
Identity does not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Leases instead of Ownership\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is one area where I'd deliberately avoid permanent leadership.\
Coordinator status is a lease.\
Leases:\
Expire.\
Renew.\
Can be relinquished.\
Can be transferred.\
Can be revoked.\
No machine permanently owns HAL.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Work Distribution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Nodes continuously advertise:\
Current load\
GPU availability\
Memory\
Latency\
Capabilities\
Battery\
Temperature\
Network quality\
Policy constraints\
HAL assigns work according to fitness rather than static assignment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Shared Knowledge\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Knowledge belongs to HAL.\
Not to nodes.\
Nodes cache.\
HAL owns.\
If a node disappears...\
Knowledge survives.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Distributed Transactions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Decision 35 introduced transactions.\
Now they become distributed.\
Long-running work may span:\
Phone\

\f7 \uc0\u8595 
\f1 \
Home server\

\f7 \uc0\u8595 
\f1 \
Cloud GPU\

\f7 \uc0\u8595 
\f1 \
NAS\

\f7 \uc0\u8595 
\f1 \
Desktop\
One transaction.\
One audit trail.\
One identity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Network Partitions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One of the hardest problems.\
Suppose:\
The home network loses Internet.\
Laptop leaves Wi-Fi.\
Cloud unavailable.\
Two GX10s disagree.\
What happens?\
Recommendation:\
When constitutional certainty is lost...\
HAL reduces capability before risking inconsistency.\
Availability never overrides integrity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Quorum\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Not every decision needs agreement.\
Examples:\
Background indexing:\
One node.\
Voice transcription:\
One node.\
Constitution change:\
Requires constitutional coordination.\
Authority-sensitive operations should require stronger guarantees than ordinary computation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Local Autonomy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Nodes shouldn't stop working simply because they are temporarily disconnected.\
Example:\
Watch.\
Phone.\
Laptop.\
They may continue local work within delegated authority.\
When reconnection occurs...\
State reconciles through transactions.\
Not blind synchronization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Conflict Resolution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Suppose two nodes independently modify the same object.\
HAL should never simply say:\
"Last writer wins."\
Instead:\
Conflicts become Decision Records.\
Evidence is preserved.\
HAL explains:\
Why the conflict occurred.\
Which authority existed.\
Which resolution policy applied.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Capability Routing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Suppose:\
Node A has the best GPU.\
Node B has the fastest SSD.\
Node C has Internet.\
Node D has privacy.\
HAL reasons:\
"Use Node A for inference.\
Node B for retrieval.\
Node C for search.\
Node D for sensitive records."\
The cluster behaves as one organism.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Health Awareness\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every node continuously publishes:\
Health\
Version\
Policy\
Latency\
Capabilities\
Storage\
Power\
Thermals\
Trust\
Coordinator eligibility\
HAL reasons over the health of the cluster.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Graceful Degradation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If 80% of nodes disappear...\
HAL doesn't "crash."\
It sheds capabilities.\
Identity persists.\
Constitution persists.\
Recovery remains possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Cluster Evolution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 New hardware joins.\
Old hardware leaves.\
Nodes upgrade.\
Nodes retire.\
The cluster evolves.\
HAL remains HAL.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Trust Between Nodes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every node authenticates every other node.\
No implicit trust because something is "inside the house."\
Nodes receive:\
Identity\
Certificates\
Trust scores\
Revocation\
Capability permissions\
Compromise isolation\
This builds directly on Decision 32.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 One concept I especially like\
