\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Proposed Constitutional Principles\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 HAL may collaborate with every trust domain, but it remains constitutionally sovereign within its own.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
And:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Cooperation expands capability. It never transfers authority.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 I think this decision completes something we've been quietly building since Decision 1. Up until now, we've defined HAL as an internally governed intelligence. Decision 49 defines how HAL behaves in a world full of 
\f2\i \strokec2 other
\f1\i0 \strokec2  intelligences.\
The architecture deliberately rejects two extremes: isolation and assimilation. HAL is neither a hermit nor a subordinate. It is a sovereign participant in a federation of trust domains, cooperating freely while never compromising the constitutional identity you've spent forty-eight decisions defining.\
One refinement I'd like to propose before we lock it is a 
\f0\b \strokec2 Constitutional Firewall
\f1\b0 \strokec2 . Every interaction that crosses a trust-domain boundary would pass through a dedicated governance layer that enforces Treaties, validates provenance, redacts unauthorized data, and records the exchange. Nothing crosses domains without that firewall's approval. I think it would become one of the defining architectural elements of HAL's external interactions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 This is fine, but I want all new treaties to be owner approved first. Maybe even founder approved? Or maybe the capabilities need to be founder approved?\
If I\'92ve never allowed Hal access to email, and my wife wants HAL to send an email on her behalf, I should need to approve that treaty and capability before she can do that.\
I think I want to be quite selective about the information that is shared to external partners before we introduce new treaties\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 I actually think you've uncovered something 
\f0\b \strokec2 more fundamental
\f1\b0 \strokec2  than treaties.\
The Constitution already answers this, if we follow it consistently.\
A treaty only defines 
\f0\b \strokec2 how two sovereign systems are allowed to interact.
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
It does 
\f0\b \strokec2 not
\f1\b0 \strokec2  determine whether HAL is allowed to possess a capability in the first place.\
Those are different decisions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
