\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The fundamental question is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 HAL cannot think about everything at once. How should it choose what deserves its attention?
\f1\b0 \strokec2 \
Humans call this attention.\
Computers call it scheduling.\
Neither is sufficient.\
HAL needs a constitutional model of focus.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Three approaches\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Option A \'97 First Come, First Served\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Handle requests in arrival order.\
Simple.\
Fair.\
But urgency, importance, and safety disappear.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Option B \'97 Priority Queues\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Assign numerical priorities.\
Execute highest first.\
Better.\
Still static.\
Priority alone doesn't explain 
\f2\i \strokec2 why
\f1\i0 \strokec2  something matters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Option C \'97 Constitutional Attention (\strokec2 My Recommendation\strokec2 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL treats 
\f0\b \strokec2 attention as a governed, limited constitutional resource
\f1\b0 \strokec2 .\
Attention is neither permanent nor equal.\
It is intentionally allocated based on evidence, responsibility, and constitutional obligations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

